From 92f65750e6503683a7462f6ef1d62bdd33fd16c2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 13:42:19 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 13/16] daemon: fetch pruned blocks through a sliding window

Every block on this path is one the local node has discarded, so each is a round
trip to the archival helper or, failing that, a peer fetch through the proxy costing
seconds rather than milliseconds. Fetched one after another, a wallet whose history
reaches back past the prune height pays that per block.

Fetch several at once, keeping a bounded window in flight and delivering in request
order as each head completes.

A window and not fixed batches, which is the whole of the design rather than a
refinement. This was first written as chunks joined before the next chunk began, and
that version had to be reverted for indexing slower than fetching serially: 571-601s
per 2000-block batch against ~488s. At a barrier one slow fetch, or one retry backing
off a whole second, leaves every other worker idle until the slowest item in the
chunk lands, so the batch costs its worst member rather than its average. Topping the
window up as each block is delivered keeps the workers fed instead.

That earlier attempt also had no connection pool, so its concurrency was not real:
`jsonrpc` holds one socket behind a mutex and serialised the fetches it thought it
was running in parallel. It therefore paid the barrier and bought nothing. The
preceding commit is what makes this worth doing at all.

Delivery stays sequential and in request order because callers accumulate state
through `func`, and the window bounds memory, which matters when blocks are megabytes
and an indexing run is thousands of them.

The window is `pipeline`, separated from the block fetching so it can be tested on
its own: that delivery follows request order and stays inside the concurrency bound,
that the first error in request order is the one returned with nothing past it
delivered, and -- the one that matters -- that work continues past an item still
outstanding. That last is written as a liveness check rather than a timing comparison
so it cannot go flaky, and it fails against the batching implementation, which is the
point of having it.
